<h1>Execution Status Frame</h1>
<p>A run has a structure: a test runs its cycles, a cycle runs its cases, a case runs its case cycles, and a case
	cycle runs flows and steps. The tree reports the outcome of that structure once the pieces finish, and the log
	reports what happens inside them. The <strong>Execution Status</strong> frame, below the log in the right
	pane, reports where in the structure the run is right now: which stage is active at each level, how far along
	it is, and how long the whole run is expected to take. It is the frame to watch during a long run.</p>

<h2 id="stages-and-progress">Stages and progress</h2>
<p>Run <strong>Status: Fail</strong> and look at the frame while the run is under way:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/execution-status-frame/status-frame-during-run.png", "Execution Status frame during the Status: Fail run with an ETA of 6 seconds and a spinner, and four stage rows: the test at 5 of 16 with a purple 25 percent bar, Test Cycle 1 at 1 of 1, Case 5 at 5 of 16, and Case Cycle 1 at 1 of 1", "large") }}
<p>Each row is one level of the run, nested like the results tree. <strong>Stage Name</strong> names the stage
	that is active at that level: the test, its cycle, the case with its parameters, the case cycle. When a flow
	or a step is running, rows for them appear below, the step's row naming the endpoint it runs on.
	<strong>Current</strong> and <strong>Total</strong> count that level's items, five of sixteen cases here, and
	the <strong>Progress</strong> bar shows the completed share. The test's bar is purple, the colour of a running
	status, and it fills as cases complete.</p>
<p>The header carries the <strong>ETA</strong>, the estimated time to the end of the run, computed from how long
	the completed items took, and the spinner that turns while the status is running. The estimate settles after
	the first few items and is the figure to glance at when deciding whether to wait.</p>

<h2 id="the-finished-run">The finished run</h2>
<p>When the run ends, the frame keeps the last state of every level and takes the final status colour:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/execution-status-frame/status-frame-after-run.png", "Execution Status frame after the Status: Fail run with an ETA of 0 seconds, the test bar full and red, and the cycle, the last case and its case cycle at 100 percent", "large") }}
<p>The test's bar is full and red, the frame's outline matches, and the rows show the last case that ran. The
	frame belongs to the selected run: selecting another run in the tree shows that run's state, and starting a
	new run resets it.</p>

<h2 id="the-collapsed-frame">The collapsed frame</h2>
<p>Click the arrow before the <strong>Execution Status</strong> title to fold the frame into one line:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/execution-status-frame/status-frame-collapsed.png", "Collapsed Execution Status frame showing the title, the name Test: Status: Fail and a single red bar at 100 percent", "large") }}
<p>The collapsed frame keeps the name of the running test and a single progress bar for it, so the run can still
	be followed while the log takes the rest of the pane:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/execution-status-frame/status-frame-collapsed-window.png", "Controller with the Execution Status frame collapsed to a single line at the bottom of the right pane and the log filling the space above it", "large") }}
<p>Click the arrow again to expand the stages.</p>

<p>This completes the tutorial's tour of the controller's frames. The tree, the run panel, the log, the status
	frame and the Details dialog are the same views an <a class="text-accent" href="/ai-agents/">AI agent</a> works
	from when it runs the suites for you, through the files the controller exports rather than through the window;
	the <a class="text-accent" href="/ai-agents/">AI Agents</a> section picks the story up from there.</p>
